    "### Exercise 1: port a program that calls a function from inside a loop\n",
    "\n",
    "**Goal:** make the loops in `device_routine.c` run on the GPU, including the call to `compute()`, and get the same answer as the CPU run above.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Open [`device_routine.c`](./C/1_device_routine/0_device_routine_portyourself/device_routine.c) and [`compute.c`](./C/1_device_routine/0_device_routine_portyourself/compute.c) to edit. Read both before you change anything.\n",
    "2. Add OpenMP pragmas to every loop that should run on the GPU, using the clauses you have learned. One of the loops has a race condition, so use what you learned in **Reductions, atomics, and mutexes** to fix it.\n",
    "3. `compute()` is called from inside a loop that runs on the GPU, so it needs to be compiled for the GPU as well. Remember the `declare target` directive from earlier in this notebook.\n",
    "4. Choose how the data reaches the GPU:\n",
    "   - **Unified shared memory** (covered in **Managed memory**): add `requires unified_shared_memory` to [`compute.c`](./C/1_device_routine/0_device_routine_portyourself/compute.c). This needs `HSA_XNACK=1`, which the run cell sets.\n",
    "   - **Explicit memory** (covered in **Explicit memory directive**): use an unstructured data region.\n",
    "5. Save each file with Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on a Mac), return to this tab, and run the compile-and-run cell above. The answer should match the CPU run.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Compare with our solutions** once you have tried it yourself:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Unified shared memory: [`device_routine.c`](./C/1_device_routine/1_device_routine_usm/device_routine.c) and [`compute.c`](./C/1_device_routine/1_device_routine_usm/compute.c)\n",
    "- Explicit memory: [`device_routine.c`](./C/1_device_routine/2_device_routine_map/device_routine.c) and [`compute.c`](./C/1_device_routine/2_device_routine_map/compute.c)"
   ]
  },
