   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8f1ac040",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Exercise 2: a function that uses global data\n",
    "\n",
    "**Goal:** port the program again, this time with a function that reads a global array.\n",
    "\n",
    "Run it on the CPU first. The result is different from Exercise 1. Looking at `compute()` shows why: the sum includes an extra term from the global `constants` array, which is defined in a different file. The GPU needs its own copy of that array.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Open [`device_routine.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/0_device_routine_wglobaldata_portyourself/device_routine.c), [`compute.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/0_device_routine_wglobaldata_portyourself/compute.c) and [`global_data.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/0_device_routine_wglobaldata_portyourself/global_data.c) to edit.\n",
    "2. Repeat the steps from Exercise 1: add the pragmas to the loops that should run on the GPU, fix the race condition, put `compute()` inside `declare target`, and choose a memory model.\n",
    "3. Put the `constants` array in a `declare target` region in [`global_data.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/0_device_routine_wglobaldata_portyourself/global_data.c).\n",
    "4. Put the `extern` declaration of `constants` in a `declare target` region in [`compute.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/0_device_routine_wglobaldata_portyourself/compute.c) as well, so both files agree that the array exists on the GPU.\n",
    "5. Save each file with Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on a Mac), return to this tab, and run the compile-and-run cell above. The answer should match the CPU run.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Compare with our solution** once you have tried it yourself: [`device_routine.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/1_device_routine_wglobaldata/device_routine.c), [`compute.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/1_device_routine_wglobaldata/compute.c) and [`global_data.c`](./C/2_device_routine_wglobaldata/1_device_routine_wglobaldata/global_data.c)."
   ]
  },
