   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Exercise 3: a function that uses dynamically allocated global data\n",
    "\n",
    "**Goal:** port the program once more. The `constants` array is now allocated at runtime, so it can't simply be placed in a `declare target` region as before.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Open [`device_routine.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/0_device_routine_wdynglobaldata_portyourself/device_routine.c), [`compute.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/0_device_routine_wdynglobaldata_portyourself/compute.c) and [`global_data.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/0_device_routine_wdynglobaldata_portyourself/global_data.c) to edit.\n",
    "2. Repeat the steps from Exercises 1 and 2: add the pragmas to the loops that should run on the GPU, fix the race condition, and put `compute()` and the global declarations inside `declare target`.\n",
    "3. Move the dynamically allocated `constants` array to the GPU. Do the copy **after** the array has been allocated and after any changes made to it on the CPU, otherwise the GPU gets stale or empty data.\n",
    "4. Save each file with Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on a Mac), return to this tab, and run the compile-and-run cell above. The answer should match the CPU run.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Compare with our solution** once you have tried it yourself: [`device_routine.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/1_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/device_routine.c), [`compute.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/1_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/compute.c) and [`global_data.c`](./C/3_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/1_device_routine_wdynglobaldata/global_data.c)."
   ]
  },
